---
title: Troubleshooting
description: Resolve toolchain, module, cache, and generated-file problems in a predictable order.
section: Reference
order: 21
updated: '2026-10-09'
---

Start with the failed task and its first error. The CMake sections below apply to C/C++; Python, Cargo, and SwiftPM use their native task output and environment. Configuration, compilation, editor diagnostics, and runtime checks establish different things.

## No Rivet project found

Commands search above the current file or working directory for `.rivet.json` or native markers: `CMakeLists.txt`, `pyproject.toml`, `Cargo.toml`, and `Package.swift`. CMake discovery prefers a project declaration or presets over a nested source-list fragment. Open a file in the project, change directory to its root, or choose an explicit CLI root:

```sh
rivet build --root ./engine
```

Native build tasks work without an Rivet manifest. File and target generation requires one; discovering an existing project does not adopt its source lists. See [Existing projects](/docs/existing-projects/).

## What Doctor checks

Run `:RivetDoctor` in the project, or `rivet doctor --root PATH`. Warnings describe missing optional tools or checks needing configuration; errors produce a nonzero CLI status. A clean report is not a substitute for Build, Test, or an actual GPU run.

| Provider or profile | Checks | Still verify by running the workflow |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C++ | Selected preset/compiler, scanner and module requirements, compilation database and clangd settings | Actual feature support, linking, tests, runtime behavior |
| C | Configured compiler from CMake File API; warns before a reply is available | Compiler standard support and actual build/test |
| Python | Selected interpreter/environment, Python 3.11+, Ruff, packaging `build` for ordinary Python, selected pytest, uv where applicable | Imports, dependency compatibility, package creation, test results |
| Rust | Cargo availability, rustfmt/Clippy commands, native target metadata | Successful compilation, lint, tests, runtime |
| Swift | Swift availability, `swift format`, native target metadata | Package compilation, tests, Apple app integration |
| OpenMP / MPI / CUDA | Relevant configured CMake compiler/runtime settings | Working runtimes, rank/thread behavior, NVCC host compatibility, actual devices |
| Metal | macOS, Xcode Metal compiler, configured header path | Shader compilation and GPU pixel check |
| Dawn / Vulkan / OpenGL | Configured SDK/loader/GLFW locations; explains runtime prerequisites | Vulkan shader tools and validation layers, working device/context, actual rendering |

Configure first when a check needs CMake cache or File API data. Doctor does not install anything. Native package metadata discovery can evaluate `Package.swift` through SwiftPM.

## CMake cannot compile the project

Run `rivet doctor` or `:RivetDoctor` and inspect the selected preset, configured compiler, scanner, and compilation database. The generated project requires the standard selected at creation; individual language features still depend on the compiler. On macOS, check that the selected compiler is the intended LLVM installation, with a compatible `clang-scan-deps`, when using modules.

Compiler paths belong in `CMakeUserPresets.json`. Rivet selects `local-debug` or `local-release` when those presets exist. Changing compiler or generator should use a fresh compatible build directory rather than reusing an incompatible CMake cache.

Either module option requires Ninja. To create a conventional Make project, specify both choices:

```sh
rivet new ./example --name example \
  --modules false --std-modules false \
  --generator "Unix Makefiles"
```

## Standard-library modules are rejected

The generated helper currently supports verified CMake versions **4.4.0 through 4.4.4**. A newer version may support imports itself but still fall outside Rivet's verified experimental gate. The error deliberately identifies that version limit.

The compiler and selected standard library must also advertise import support for the selected C++23 or C++26 mode. A metadata error means CMake cannot find that toolchain's installed module manifest. Set `CMAKE_CXX_STDLIB_MODULES_JSON` in the appropriate local user preset when automatic discovery is unavailable. Use metadata belonging to the selected compiler/library installation; an unrelated manifest is not a substitute.

Rivet preserves explicit metadata settings, including an invalid path, so inspect existing overrides before adding another. See [Modules and headers](/docs/modules/) for the supported arrangement.

## clangd cannot find a module

Build your selected preset first; for example:

```sh
rivet build --preset debug
```

Rivet refreshes `.cache/rivet/compile_commands.json` from the selected build after configure. Doctor identifies a missing or stale copy. Custom `.clangd` files are preserved; set `CompilationDatabase: .cache/rivet` if you want them to follow Rivet. Module artifacts are produced by the build, so configuration alone may leave `module 'std' not found` diagnostics.

Check that clangd has module support enabled and uses the intended toolchain. Its module support is experimental; the existing Neovim LSP configuration remains responsible for starting it. Restart clangd if diagnostics persist after generating previously missing artifacts.

Compare editor diagnostics with an actual build before changing working code solely to satisfy the language server. A missing declaration in clangd does not by itself prove that the compiler or standard library lacks that feature. The starter uses `std::cout`; this is not a general restriction on using `std::println`. See [clangd’s module-support status](https://clangd.llvm.org/features#experimental-c20-modules-support).

## A generated-file update is refused

Rivet refuses existing destination files, edited owned fragments, and files changed since preview. The creation-time exception is `.gitignore`: generated rules are prepended and your existing rules are preserved in the review. Inspect any conflicting path. Preserve custom CMake in the root `CMakeLists.txt` or an included custom file, then reconcile the manifest and generated fragment before retrying. Do not discard a newer edit just to dismiss the conflict.

## Git setup failed

Git initialization is optional. An explicit request requires Git on `PATH`; if it is missing, creation stops before writing. Rivet reuses existing repositories and worktrees instead of creating a nested repository. If that repository changes while a review is open, start a fresh preview.

If the error says the project files were saved but Git setup failed, fix the reported Git issue and run `git init` inside the created folder. Repeating `RivetNew` would encounter the files that were already created. Rivet does not stage or commit files as part of creation.

An interrupted process can leave `.rivet-lock`. Confirm no Rivet write is active before removing that empty lock directory.

## Tests or formatting are unavailable

CTest runs the starter's test executable; Catch2 is not automatically installed. Check `BUILD_TESTING` if the test workflow reports no tests. The generated `format` target exists only when CMake finds `clang-format`; install or expose the formatter, then configure again.

## Recover an edited managed file

Suppose you added `target_compile_definitions(simulation_core PUBLIC TRACE=1)` directly to `cmake/RivetTargets.cmake`. The next Rivet file operation stops because that file differs from its recorded snapshot.

1. Run `:RivetRepair` and select `cmake/RivetTargets.cmake`.
2. Review the restoration and a timestamped `.rivet-recovery/.../cmake/RivetTargets.cmake` copy containing your edits.
3. Apply with `c`. The edited copy is preserved; a stale preview is refused.
4. Move the intended `target_compile_definitions` line into the root `CMakeLists.txt` after the generated includes. Retry the original file operation and build/test.

The CLI shows the same plan with `rivet repair cmake/RivetTargets.cmake --preview`. Omit `--preview` to apply it. Repair restores the recorded version; it does not infer how an independently edited manifest and generated file should be merged. Compare the backup and manifest if both were edited.

## Native tools and hardware

Check Doctor's coverage above and the selected environment before installing optional tools. SDK cache entries alone do not prove the entire SDK or runtime works. [Python](/docs/python/), [Rust](/docs/rust/), [SwiftPM](/docs/swift/), and [graphics](/docs/graphics/) explain prerequisites and their first successful run.

A generated GPU **test executable** returns 77 when hardware/context is unavailable. Its `SKIP_RETURN_CODE` property makes CTest report it as skipped; **CTest itself can exit 0 even when that GPU test did not run**. Inspect the skipped-test list or your CI's CTest results, not just the command status or aggregate percentage. Build errors and wrong readback pixels remain failures. [CMake skip-return-code behavior](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/prop_test/SKIP_RETURN_CODE.html).

## Swift package customization

Adding a Swift source with `:RivetFile` preserves a customized `Package.swift`. Builds, tests, and Run continue to use SwiftPM's native manifest. Adding a target through Rivet needs a generated-manifest update and stops if you have edited that manifest.

To keep custom dependencies or platform settings, add new products and targets directly in `Package.swift`. Native task discovery sees those changes. If you also change source paths or target names, use those native paths yourself; the generated file picker retains its recorded target directories.

If you deliberately want the original generated manifest back:

1. Save `Package.swift`, run `:RivetRepair`, and select it.
2. Review both the restoration and the `.rivet-recovery/.../Package.swift` backup containing your customization.
3. Apply only if restoring that snapshot is intended, then build and test.

Repair does not merge dependencies, platforms, products, or targets. Reapplying custom settings makes the manifest customized again; Rivet target generation will again refuse to replace it. Unlike CMake, there is no separate `CMakeLists.txt` extension location for Swift package settings.
